===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|entomology}} An [[insect]] of the [[order]] {{taxfmt|Hemiptera|order}} (the “true bugs”).
# Any of various species of marine or freshwater [[crustacean]]s; e.g. a [[Moreton Bay bug]], [[mudbug]].
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|date=Feb 2021|journal= The Road Ahead|location=Brisbane|page=39|column=2|text='''Bugs''', oysters, prawns and crabs {{...}} are plated up on the decks of four side-by-side trawlers bobbing on the calm waters of Trinity Inlet.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Any [[insect]], [[arachnid]], or other terrestrial [[arthropod]] that is a [[pest]].
#: {{ux|en|These flies are a bother. I’ll get some '''bug''' spray and kill them.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Any [[minibeast]].
# {{lb|en|US}} Any [[insect]], [[arachnid]], [[myriapod]] or [[entognath]].
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete|specifically}} A [[bedbug]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1874|author=Henry Sampson|title=A history of advertising|page=278
|passage=Speaking of advertising changes of name, a title by which those lodging-house pests, '''bugs''', are now often known, that of [[Norfolk Howard]]s, is derived from an advertisement in which one Ephraim Bug avowed his intention of being for the future known as Norfolk Howard.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1910|author=Aristotle|title=The Works of Aristotle: Historia animalium|translator=D.W. Thompson
|text='''Bugs''' are generated from the moisture of living animals, as it dries up outside their bodies. Lice are generated out of the flesh of animals.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q179550}}{{lb|en|chiefly|_|computing|engineering|_|jargon}} A [[problem]] that needs [[fixing]].
#: {{syn|en|defect|glitch}}
#: {{ux|en|The software '''bug''' led the computer to calculate 2 plus 2 as 3.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1878|year_published=1989|quotee=w:Thomas Edison|author=Thomas P. Hughes| quoted_in=American Genesis: A History of the American Genius for Invention| title=Edison to Puskas, 13 November 1878, Edison papers|deriv=cited by| isbn=0-14-009741-4| publisher=Penguin Books| location=Edison National Laboratory, U.S. National Park Service, West Orange, N.J.| page=75| passage=I have the right principle and am on the right track, but time, hard work and some good luck are necessary too. It has been just so in all of my inventions. The first step is an intuition, and comes with a burst, then difficulties arise — this thing gives out and [it is] then that "'''Bugs'''" — as such little faults and difficulties are called — show themselves and months of intense watching, study and labor are requisite before commercial success or failure is certainly reached.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Popular Mechanics|date=April 1968| passage=A... leading aluminum producer claims it has worked all the '''bugs''' out of building and servicing aluminum radiators, says it hopes to have a large chunk of the radiator market by the early nineteen seventies.}}
# A contagious [[illness]], or a [[pathogen]] causing it.
#: {{ux|en|He's got the flu '''bug'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} An [[enthusiasm]] for something; an [[obsession]].
#: {{ux|en|I caught the skiing '''bug''' while staying in the Alps.}}
#* {{RQ:Kerouac On the Road|part=1|chapter=1|passage=As we rode in the bus in the weird phosphorescent void of the Lincoln Tunnel we leaned on each other with fingers waving and yelled and talked excitedly, and I was beginning to get the '''bug''' like Dean.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A keen [[enthusiast]] or [[hobbyist]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1961|author=Fredric Brown|title=Nightmare in Yellow
|passage=His mother had been a '''bug''' on astrology, which was why the moment of his birth had been impressed on him so exactly.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|journal=Kiplinger's Personal Finance|volume=15|issue=12|page=34
|passage=Incidentally, the camera manufacturers have had a new worry—that they might "kill off the hobby," as U.S. Camera magazine put it recently—by automating to the point that real camera '''bugs''' would feel no challenge.}}
# A [[concealed]] [[electronic]] [[eavesdropping]] or [[intercept]] [[device]].
#: {{ux|en|We installed a '''bug''' in her telephone.}}
# A small and usually [[invisible]] [[file]] (traditionally a single-[[pixel]] [[image]]) on a [[World Wide Web]] page, primarily used to [[track]] [[user]]s.
#: {{ux|en|He suspected the image was a Web '''bug''' used for determining who was visiting the site.}}
# {{lb|en|Maine}} A [[lobster]].
# {{lb|en|broadcasting}} A small, usually [[transparent]] or [[translucent]] [[image]] placed in a corner of a [[television]] program to identify the broadcasting [[network]] or [[cable]] [[channel]].
#: {{ux|en|Channel 4's '''bug''' distracted Jim from his favorite show.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation}} A [[manually]] [[position]]ed [[marker]] in [[flight instrument]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2004|journal=Flying Magazine|volume=131|issue=10|page=10
|passage=You look up the proper speed for the phase of flight, set the reminder '''bug''', and then literally forget the speed. You don't read the airspeed number, you fly to the '''bug'''.}}
# A semi-automated [[telegraph key]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|author=Paul Gallico|title=Farewell to Sport|page=257|passage=At this point your telegraph operator, sitting at your right, goes "Ticky-tick-tickety-de-tick-tick," with his '''bug''', as he calls his transmitter, and looks at you expectantly.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1942|author=Arthur Reinhold Nilson|title=Radio Code Manual|page=134|passage=As far as the dashes are concerned, the '''bug''' is the same in operation as any regular key would be if it were turned up on edge instead of sitting flat on the desk.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=E. L. Doctorow|title=World's Fair|page=282|passage=I was a very good radio operator. I bought my own '''bug'''. That's what the telegraph key in its modern form was called. It was semiautomatic.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[hobgoblin|Hobgoblin]], [[scarecrow]]; anything that terrifies. {{defdate|late 14th c.–early 17th. c}}
#: {{syn|en|bog|bogey|bogle|boggle|boggard|bugbear}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=III|scene=ii|passage=Sir, spare your threats: / The '''bug''' which you would fright me with I seek.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|LGBTQ|"the bug"}} [[HIV]].
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2019
|author=Tora Holmberg; Annika Jonsson; Fredrik Palm
|title=Death Matters: Cultural Sociology of Mortal Life
|publisher=Springer
|isbn=9783030114855
|page=130
|passage=The arguably most debated bareback practice that came to attract attention early on (and still does) was that of "'''bug''' chasing," in which HIV-negative men ('''bug''' chasers) actively seek out sex with HIV-positive men (gift givers).}}
# {{lb|en|poker}} A limited form of [[wild card]] in some variants of [[poker]].
# {{lb|en|paleontology|slang}} A [[trilobite]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Kirk Johnson|title=Cruisin' the Fossil Freeway|page=174
|passage=We asked Harris if he had any recommendations about seeing the famous trilobite digs. He said we should just drive out to his claim in the Wheeler Quadrangle, and it was just fine with him if we dug a few '''bugs'''.}}
# {{lb|en|petroleum industry|slang|dated}} {{synonym of|en|oil bug}}.
#* '''July 1933''', ''Popular Science'':
#*: Now, only three years later, most of the major oil companies maintain staffs of these men who examine cores, classify the various types of "'''bugs'''," or foraminifera, and make charts showing the depths at which each of the hundreds of types is found.
# {{lb|en|slang|US|horse-racing}} An [[asterisk]] denoting an [[apprentice]] [[jockey]]'s weight allowance.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Anita Scialli|title=Inside Track 1999|page=62
|passage=The "'''bugs'''" are the asterisks next to the apprentice's name. One '''bug''' is a five-pound allowance, two '''bugs''' equal seven pounds, and three '''bugs''' equal ten pounds.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|US|horse-racing|by extension}} A [[young]] [[apprentice]] [[jockey]].
#: {{syn|en|bug boy}}
# {{lb|en|printing}} {{synonym of|en|union bug}}.
# {{lb|en|gambling|slang}} A small piece of [[metal]] used in a [[slot machine]] to block certain winning combinations.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1961|author=John Scarne|title=Complete Guide to Gambling|page=394
|passage=Because many illegal slot-machine operators here and abroad do not like to give the slot-machine player even one chance to hit the jackpot or the big bonus, they make use of a "'''bug'''." This is a small, flat half-circle of iron about an inch long, which looks something like a bug.}}
# {{lb|en|gambling|slang}} A metal clip attached to the underside of a table, etc. to hold hidden cards, as a form of cheating.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1897|author=Robert Frederick Foster|title=Foster's Complete Hoyle|page=195
|passage=Some clumsy or audacious sharpers will go so far as to hold out cards in their lap, or stick them in a "'''bug'''" under the table.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=Jon Sharpe|title=The Trailsman #299: Dakota Danger
|passage=Fargo had been in a saloon in Kansas when a man was caught using a '''bug'''. Made of steel and shaped like a money clip with two sharp ends, the '''bug''' was jammed under a table and held cards the bug's owner palmed until they were needed.}}

